      <div style="color:var(--muted);font-size:0.85rem;margin-bottom:8px;">Comparisons &middot; November 11, 2026 &middot; 6 min read</div>
      <h1 style="font-size:1.8rem;line-height:1.2;">California Air Tools vs Porter-Cable: Which Compressor?</h1>
    </div>

    <p>The short answer: if you are inflating tires and running a nailer, the Porter-Cable C2002 at $149.99 does everything you need. If you want to run an impact wrench or a die grinder without stopping to wait for the tank, you need the California Air Tools 10020C at $412.50, and no smaller compressor will substitute.</p>

    <p>That is a $262 difference and it comes down to one spec that most buyers never read.</p>
<a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=California+Air+Tools+10020C&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored" class="btn" style="margin:16px 0 24px;display:inline-block;">Check Price on Amazon</a>
<a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=Porter+Cable+C2002+air+compressor&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored" class="btn" style="margin:16px 0 24px;display:inline-block;">Check Price on Amazon</a>

    <h2>CFM Is the Number, Not Tank Size</h2>

    <p>Air tools do not care how big your tank is. They care how much air the pump can deliver while the tool is running. That number is CFM at 90 psi, and it is the only spec that tells you what a compressor can actually do.</p>

    <table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;margin:24px 0;font-size:0.92rem;">
      <thead>
        <tr style="border-bottom:2px solid var(--border);text-align:left;">
          <th style="padding:8px 6px;">Spec</th>
