    </table>

    <p>Now put those CFM numbers next to what tools demand at 90 psi:</p>

    <p><strong>1/2-inch air impact wrench:</strong> roughly 4 to 6 CFM.<br/>
    <strong>Air ratchet:</strong> roughly 3 to 5 CFM.<br/>
    <strong>Die grinder or cutoff tool:</strong> roughly 5 to 10 CFM.<br/>
    <strong>Blow gun, nailer, tire inflation:</strong> 1 to 2 CFM, bursty.</p>

    <p>That is the whole decision. The Porter-Cable delivers 2.6 CFM. A tire, a blow gun, and a finish nailer fit under that. An impact wrench does not, and a die grinder is not close. You can still bolt an impact wrench onto the pancake. It will rattle for four seconds, die, and you will stand there listening to the pump, which is the experience that makes people think pneumatic tools are bad.</p>

    <h2>What Tank Size Is Actually For</h2>

    <p>A bigger tank is a buffer, not a power source. Ten gallons at 5.3 CFM means you can put down a long continuous pull before the motor ever kicks on, and when it does kick on, the pump refills fast enough that you barely notice.</p>

    <p>Six gallons at 2.6 CFM is a small buffer being drained faster than it can fill. This is why the pancake feels fine for the first tire and progressively worse for the fourth.</p>

    <p>It is also why a bigger tank on a weak pump does not solve anything. CFM first, tank second, every time.</p>

    <h2>Noise and Duty Cycle, the Two Things Nobody Mentions</h2>

    <p>The California Air is rated 70 dB, which is the difference between wearing hearing protection and being able to talk to someone while it runs. That matters more than it sounds like it should. An oil-free pancake in a closed garage is genuinely unpleasant, and the annoyance is why so many of them end up on a shelf.</p>

    <p>The oil-free pump also has a duty cycle, meaning it needs to rest. An oil-lubed cast-iron pump runs cooler and longer. If you are using the compressor for 30 seconds at a time, an oil-free unit is perfect. If you are using it for an hour, oil-lubed exists for that reason.</p>

    <p>Either way, drain the tank. Water condenses in there, and moisture is what kills air tools and ruins a paint job. A filter and regulator downstream of the tank is $25 well spent.</p>

    <h2>Verdict</h2>

    <p><strong>Buy the Porter-Cable C2002</strong> if your compressor is for tires, a blow gun, and a finish nailer, and you want to carry it to the driveway one handed. At $149.99 and 30 pounds, it is still the correct first compressor for most home garages.</p>

    <p><strong>Buy the California Air Tools 10020C</strong> the moment you want a real impact wrench or any continuous air tool. 5.3 CFM is what makes pneumatic tools work instead of tease you, and 70 dB is what makes you willing to turn it on.</p>

    <p>One honest thing: if you are buying a compressor specifically for lug nuts, price a cordless impact first. An M18-class cordless makes 1,400 ft-lb and needs no tank, no hose, and no drain valve. Air wins when you need a grinder or a spray gun. It is not automatically the right answer for a wheel.</p>

    <p>See the full field, including the quiet 12-gallon Stealth and the oil-lubed Makita Big Bore, on the <a href="/tools/air-compressor" style="color:var(--accent);">air compressor comparison</a>, or read the <a href="/posts/best-air-compressor-review" style="color:var(--accent);">full air compressor review</a>. For the wheel end of the job, the <a href="/posts/best-impact-wrench-review" style="color:var(--accent);">impact wrench review</a> covers the cordless options. And if you are working under a car at all, put it on <a href="/tools/car-ramps" style="color:var(--accent);">ramps</a> or <a href="/tools/jack-stand" style="color:var(--accent);">jack stands</a> before you start.</p>
